<rml>
<head>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="theme.rcss"/>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="newchar_common.rcss"/>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="sidebar_hud.rcss"/>
</head>
<body data-model="sidebar_hud">
    <!-- ==================================================================
         SIDEBAR HUD — the character creator's register
         ==================================================================

         THIS DOCUMENT SPEAKS THE CREATOR'S VOCABULARY, NOT ITS OWN.

         `newchar_common.rcss` is linked above `sidebar_hud.rcss` and every
         device on screen comes out of it unchanged: `.nc-colhead` +
         `.nc-col-sigil` + `.nc-colname` for a panel head, `.nc-rule` for a
         separator, `.nc-pip` for a meter, `.nc-fact-*` for a labelled value,
         `.nc-legend-*` for a `LABEL :: value` pair, `.nc-chip` for a flag,
         `.nc-tally-*` for the effects roster. Linking that sheet costs nothing
         the HUD does not use — every rule in it is class-scoped, there is not
         one bare-element selector in the file — and it is what makes the two
         surfaces impossible to drift apart. Do not copy a creator rule into
         `sidebar_hud.rcss` to tweak it; override the property you need there
         and leave the device where it lives.

         The HUD it replaced was a character-cell terminal, and its frames were
         real box-drawing glyphs emitted from C++ because a CSS border cannot
         pick a junction glyph. That constraint is gone with the grid: every
         frame here is a `border` or a `.nc-rule` div, and no producer emits a
         `─ │ ┌ ┐ └ ┘ ├ ┤ ┬ ┴ ┼` ever again. See `plans/hud-creator-register.md`;
         `plans/phosphor-hud.md` is the record of the register this retired.

         WHY EXACTLY TWO REGIONS ARE OPAQUE.

         Hierarchy is still luminance: the six-rung `hud-i0`..`hud-i5` ladder is
         the only thing a producer may colour with, and every gold mark on
         screen comes from this stylesheet and is redundant with a shape. A
         luminance encoding is only readable over a background whose luminance
         is controlled, so the two regions holding state a player must never
         misread carry an opaque ground: `#hud-status` (safe mode, hostiles,
         stats, needs) and `#hud-keys` (which action is on which key, and which
         are unavailable — a step down to `hud-i1`, precisely the distinction an
         uncontrolled backdrop would eat).

         Everything else floats over live terrain behind `{{hud-veil}}`.

         AND WHY THAT IS AN OCCLUSION SAVING, NOT A COSMETIC CHOICE.

         Floating means floating: the veiled panels are drawn on top of the map,
         they are NOT carved out of the terrain viewport. Only the two opaque
         strips are (`sidebar_hud_top_rows` / `_bottom_rows`), because only they
         hide what is under them.

         TREE ORDER IS PAINT ORDER. The three overlays come last so the CRT
         glass and the damage flash sit over the HUD as well as over the map.

         Panel titles are authored in sentence case and upper-cased by
         `.nc-colname`'s `text-transform`, so no locale ships a second copy of a
         word purely to shout it. -->

    <!-- STATUS — full width, top edge, opaque. Two rows of world and character
         state with a dotted rule between them. Each row's three groups are
         emitted by its producer; the middle group wraps and clips, so a narrow
         viewport drops trailing fields instead of overrunning. -->
    <div id="hud-status" class="hud-strip">
        <div class="hud-meta" data-rml="status_row1_rml"></div>
        <div class="nc-rule"></div>
        <div class="hud-meta" data-rml="status_row2_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- SOMA — left column, translucent. Body parts with their pip meters, the
         pools, and the per-part effect roster. Left-hand because a critical limb
         is the first thing a left-to-right reader must hit, and its effect chips
         sit on the row directly under the meter they describe. -->
    <div id="hud-soma" class="hud-panel">
        <div class="nc-colhead">
            <div class="nc-col-sigil hud-sigil-soma"></div>
            <span class="nc-colname">Soma</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hud-body" data-rml="soma_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- RADAR — right column, top, and the one panel with no ground of its own.
         Its content is the dot minimap, drawn as GPU quads on the UI compositing
         layer UNDER this document; a veil over it would multiply the two dimmest
         dot categories into the ground. `hud_radar::draw` paints its own
         `ink::ground` fill instead, and this element contributes the border and
         the head. The head keeps a veil of its own so it is not left sitting
         unreadable on live terrain. -->
    <div id="hud-radar" class="hud-panel hud-panel-clear">
        <div class="nc-colhead">
            <div class="nc-col-sigil hud-sigil-radar"></div>
            <span class="nc-colname">Overmap</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DOCK — right column under the radar, translucent. Mission marker,
         target, arms. Mirrors to the left column with SIDEBAR_POSITION, handled
         entirely by `layout_for`'s `sidebar_right`; this document holds no
         side-specific markup, and the stylesheet holds no side-specific edge
         fade for the same reason. -->
    <div id="hud-dock" class="hud-panel">
        <div class="nc-colhead">
            <div class="nc-col-sigil hud-sigil-dock"></div>
            <span class="nc-colname">Dock</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hud-body" data-rml="dock_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- LOG — bottom-left above the keys strip, translucent, and sized to
         exactly the number of message rows it is showing. No fixed well: that is
         what let the shipping HUD hold 267 dp of message in 752 dp of reserved
         column. Recency is the luminance ramp across the rows; the newest line
         also carries the gold left edge that is this register's cursor. -->
    <div id="hud-log" class="hud-panel">
        <div class="nc-colhead">
            <div class="nc-col-sigil hud-sigil-log"></div>
            <span class="nc-colname">Message log</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hud-body" data-rml="log_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- KEYS — full width, bottom edge, opaque. `KEY :: ACTION` pairs in the
         creator's own sigil-strip device, wrapping rather than truncating; the
         strip is anchored to the viewport's bottom edge exactly, which is what
         `sidebar_hud_bottom_rows` carves against. -->
    <div id="hud-keys" class="hud-strip">
        <div class="nc-legend" data-rml="keys_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- VEHICLE — right column under the dock, translucent. `display: none`
         unless the avatar is controlling a vehicle; the chassis toggles it. -->
    <div id="hud-vehicle" class="hud-panel">
        <div class="nc-colhead">
            <div class="nc-col-sigil hud-sigil-veh"></div>
            <span class="nc-colname">Vehicle</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hud-body" data-rml="veh_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- CRT scanline glass — full-screen, no content. A repeating gradient weak
         enough (mean ~2.5% darkening) that the tilemap stays crisp. Its job is to
         make the HUD and the world share one emissive surface instead of looking
         like text pasted onto a game. -->
    <div id="hud-crt-scan"></div>

    <!-- CRT faceplate vignette — full-screen, no content. Radial darkening
         towards the corners, the honest half of a curved-glass tube: barrel
         distortion needs a shader, corner falloff does not. Above the scanline
         layer so the falloff dims the scanlines too, as a real faceplate does. -->
    <div id="hud-crt-curve"></div>

    <!-- Damage flash — full-screen, no content. Pre-existing behaviour, driven
         by `hud_anim` on total-HP loss. Last of all, so a hit is legible over the
         CRT glass. -->
    <div id="hud-vignette"></div>
</body>
</rml>
